Seed a context checkpoint on slot-restore so SWA/hybrid/recurrent models
can REUSE a restored prompt instead of re-prefilling it.

Applied as an anchored insertion (see deploy/apply-llama-patches.sh) rather
than a line-numbered diff, so it survives minor llama.cpp drift around the
restore handler. Built at the commit the box's base image ships (read off the
base's own `llama-server --version` at build time; Dockerfile.local-llm's
LLAMA_CPP_COMMIT is only the fallback). The restore success path is worded
differently across llama.cpp versions, so the apply script knows every wording
as an alternative anchor and picks whichever the checked-out tree contains.

Anchor lines (the LAST statement of the restore success path — whichever is
present must be unique in tools/server/server-context.cpp):
    slot->prompt.tokens.insert(tokens);            (base b10068 / 571d0d540)
    slot->prompt.tokens = std::move(restored);     (b10603+ deserialize rework)

Inserted immediately after it:

                        // --- JBrain patch: reuse-on-restore for SWA/hybrid/recurrent ---
                        // A slot-restore rebuilds the memory state and repopulates
                        // slot->prompt.tokens, but for a SWA/hybrid/recurrent model the
                        // completion-path prompt reuse is gated on a CONTEXT CHECKPOINT.
                        // With none registered the reuse hits do_reset and re-prefills the
                        // whole prompt. Seed one checkpoint spanning the restored prompt so
                        // the restore is actually reusable (harmless for pure-attention).
                        if (params_base.n_ctx_checkpoints > 0 && slot->prompt.tokens.size() > 0) {
                            const auto ckpt_pos_min = llama_memory_seq_pos_min(llama_get_memory(ctx_tgt), slot->id);
                            const auto ckpt_pos_max = llama_memory_seq_pos_max(llama_get_memory(ctx_tgt), slot->id);
                            if (ckpt_pos_min >= 0 && ckpt_pos_max >= ckpt_pos_min) {
                                auto & cur = slot->prompt.checkpoints.emplace_back();
                                cur.id_task = -1;  // not owned by a completion task
                                cur.update_pos((int64_t) slot->prompt.tokens.size(), ckpt_pos_min, ckpt_pos_max);
                                cur.update_tgt(ctx_tgt, slot->id, LLAMA_STATE_SEQ_FLAGS_PARTIAL_ONLY);
                                cur.update_dft(ctx_dft, slot->id, LLAMA_STATE_SEQ_FLAGS_PARTIAL_ONLY);
                                common_speculative_get_state(spec.get(), slot->id, cur.data_spec);
                                SLT_TRC(*slot, "seeded restore checkpoint (n_tokens = %d, pos_min = %d, pos_max = %d)\n",
                                    (int) slot->prompt.tokens.size(), (int) ckpt_pos_min, (int) ckpt_pos_max);
                            }
                        }
                        // --- end JBrain patch ---

Why each line is the way it is (all verified against 571d0d540 sources; a
first draft got three of these wrong and would have failed to compile or,
worse, compiled into a no-op):

  - update_pos's FIRST argument is n_tokens — the token count the checkpoint's
    KV data covers — not a prefix offset. The reuse path clamps
    `n_past = min(size_up_to_pos(pos_next), it->n_tokens)`, so passing 0 here
    seeds a checkpoint that "restores" to n_past = 0: a full re-prefill, i.e.
    the patch would be inert. After a restore the whole prompt is in the KV,
    so the count is slot->prompt.tokens.size().
  - pos_min/pos_max are read from the model's own memory
    (llama_memory_seq_pos_min/max), mirroring create_checkpoint's caller —
    for SWA/hybrid memory the floor is not necessarily 0, and hardcoding
    (0, N-1) overclaims what the checkpoint covers. pos_min < 0 means the
    restore left no usable range: seed nothing.
  - SLT_TRC takes the slot by VALUE-ish expansion — the macro expands
    `(slot).id` — and `slot` here is a `server_slot *`, so it must be
    `SLT_TRC(*slot, ...)` or the build fails.
  - common_speculative_get_state null-checks its spec argument internally
    (speculative.cpp), so no guard is needed, matching create_checkpoint.
  - id_task = -1 because a restore runs as a slot-action task: there is no
    owning completion task, and create_checkpoint's `slot.task->id` would
    dereference null here — which is why the block mirrors that function
    rather than calling it.
